<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrich Wilhelm von Haugwitz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1702–1765</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrich Wilhelm von Haugwitz was a Saxon-born statesman and the chief architect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>'s administrative reforms after the loss of Silesia. From 1749 he merged the Austrian and Bohemian chancelleries into a single central directory, won ten-year tax grants from the estates that also taxed noble land, and used them to pay for a permanent standing army. He later presided over the Council of State created in 1760.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-wilhelm-von-haugwitz.jpg" alt="Friedrich Wilhelm von Haugwitz" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Michael Militz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_Wilhelm_von_Haugwitz_3.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
